From c7d7bd994e2030ad3a71486fab7fd47956a9edaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LiBr <me@nvme0n1p.dev>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:13:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/47] Z 6III: the body MCU, modelled and wired to the EXPEEDs

The machine so far had one processor. The body has two: an A53 cluster
running the RTOS and a Cortex-M4 that owns power, switches, the lens and
the battery, wired to the EXPEED over two serial-bus instances with a
GPIO handshake. This adds the rest of the board's device models and that
second processor, run from its own image beside the A53s, and connects
the two so they talk:

- mcu-image= runs the body MCU's image (ex2010_0200a0.bi) on a
  Cortex-M4 beside the A53s, needing -smp 4,maxcpus=5 and 1 KiB pages
  (hw/arm/nikon-z63-stm32.h, with a timer that gives the firmware its
  compare interrupts).
- mcu-spi-peer= bridges the MCU's SPI<n> to this board's serial instance
  <m>; mcu-wires= names the handshake wires as <MCU port><pin>=a<port>:<bit>
  and drives them both ways, including the MCU's output edges into the
  EXPEED's EXT_INT block. Defaults are the wiring the two firmwares' own
  pin tables name: PB12->port3 bit2, PE3->port3 bit3, PD12->port4 bit3,
  and EXPEED pin 54 = port4 bit7 -> MCU PG6 (the MCU takes it as EXTI
  line 6, whose callback posts the transmit loop's "link established"
  flag).
- The other device models the board needs: EXS1780 out (superseded),
  dev199, gfx, hdmac, i2c, imu, ispfam, nand, noc, sio, stream154c,
  wlsd, z9lab, raw pixels and the stm32.

Two model bugs stood between the two chips talking, both found by
running the real images and fixed here: with mcu-image= the synthetic
peer flag stayed set, so the EXPEED's handshake pins were answered by an
object nothing had initialised; and the MCU's SPI moves byte-sized DMA
items while the EXPEED shifts 16-bit words, so a word has to be fed as
two items (and two read back). With both fixed the version exchange
crosses both ways and the RTOS moves on.

notes/analysis for this work live outside this tree (analysis/emulation
in the nikon checkout); z63-tools/gui.sh runs the whole board.
